Q1. What are Story Points, and why are they used instead of estimating
tasks in hours?

Interview Ready Answer

Story Points are a relative unit used by the Development Team to
estimate the effort, complexity, and uncertainty involved in
implementing a User Story.

They are preferred over hours because different developers work at
different speeds. Story Points provide a consistent relative estimation
independent of individual productivity.

Definition

Story Points are a relative estimation unit.

Purpose

• Estimate effort.
• Estimate complexity.
• Estimate uncertainty.
• Help Sprint Planning.
• Predict team capacity.

======================================================================

Q2. What is Planning Poker, and how does it help estimate Story Points?

Interview Ready Answer

Planning Poker is a consensus-based estimation technique used by the
Development Team to estimate Story Points.

The Product Owner explains the User Story, and each developer
independently selects a Story Point estimate. All estimates are revealed
simultaneously. If there is a significant difference, the team discusses
the reasons and reaches a consensus.

Definition

Planning Poker is a Story Point estimation technique.

Purpose

• Independent estimation.
• Avoid estimation bias.
• Reach team consensus.
• Improve estimation accuracy.

Planning Poker Flow

Product Owner explains User Story
          ↓
Developers estimate independently
          ↓
Everyone reveals cards together
          ↓
Large difference?
      Yes ↓      No ↓
 Discussion     Final Estimate
          ↓
     Team Consensus

======================================================================

Q3. Explain the Sprint Planning process from Product Backlog to Sprint
Backlog.

Interview Ready Answer

During Sprint Planning, the Product Owner presents the prioritized
Product Backlog. The Development Team selects the Product Backlog items
that can realistically be completed in the upcoming Sprint based on
their capacity and estimates.

The selected items become the Sprint Backlog, and the team defines the
Sprint Goal.

Purpose

• Select Sprint work.
• Create Sprint Backlog.
• Define Sprint Goal.
• Plan achievable work.

Flow

Product Backlog
        ↓
Sprint Planning
        ↓
Selected Items
        ↓
Sprint Backlog
        ↓
Development

======================================================================

Q4. What is Velocity in Agile, and how is it used in Sprint Planning?

Interview Ready Answer

Velocity is the total number of Story Points completed by the
Development Team in a Sprint.

It is used during Sprint Planning to estimate how much work the team
can realistically commit to in the next Sprint based on previous Sprint
performance.

Definition

Velocity = Total completed Story Points in one Sprint.

Purpose

• Predict Sprint capacity.
• Prevent overcommitment.
• Improve Sprint Planning.

Example

Sprint 1 : 28 Story Points
Sprint 2 : 30 Story Points

Expected Velocity ≈ 29 Story Points

Next Sprint should plan around 29 Story Points.

======================================================================

Q5. What is a Burndown Chart, and what information does it provide?

Interview Ready Answer

A Burndown Chart is a graphical representation of the remaining Story
Points versus time during a Sprint.

It helps the Scrum Team track Sprint progress and determine whether they
are on schedule to complete the Sprint Goal.

Definition

Burndown Chart tracks remaining work.

Purpose

• Monitor Sprint progress.
• Compare actual progress with planned progress.
• Identify delays early.
• Predict Sprint completion.

Example

Total Story Points = 20

Day 1 → 20 Remaining
Day 2 → 17 Remaining
Day 3 → 14 Remaining
...
Day 10 → 0 Remaining

If 0 is reached before Day 10:
• Sprint completed early.

If Story Points remain after Day 10:
• Sprint Goal not achieved.

